add(3,'last-crossing','The Last Crossing','an invented Grassrivers ferry shelter','Jason: olive overshirt, white tee, dark jeans; Lucia: burgundy rain jacket, black trousers','one yellow fare envelope and one wet ferry timetable',
"Lucia bought the last crossing, then watched the ferryman sell the same two seats to somebody else.|The other passengers carried luggage. Jason carried an envelope that suddenly felt much too heavy for paper.|Lucia checked the timetable. The last boat had left before they arrived. This one was staying tied.|Across the creek, headlights appeared between trees. Their anonymous buyer had chosen a meeting with no exit.|Jason reached for the money. Lucia reached for the waiting family's bags and carried them back uphill.|The buyer flashed his lights, impatient. Jason finally understood: witnesses were supposed to leave on that boat.|Lucia handed their fare to the family. Everyone waited together beneath the shelter until the headlights vanished.|At sunrise, the real ferry arrived. They crossed without the deal. Jason called the lost money cheap.",
"Lucia at ticket window, two matching fare stubs visible|Jason beside Lucia, envelope held low, anonymous family behind|Lucia compares wet timetable to tied ferry under amber bulb|Two distant headlights across creek; heroes foreground beneath shelter|Lucia lifts family bag with both hands, Jason stays beside envelope|Headlight reflection in creek, Jason looks from ferry to family|Wide shelter with heroes and anonymous family together; headlights recede|Real passenger ferry moored at dawn, heroes board empty handed",
'incomplete transport schedule exposes a witness-isolation trap')

add(3,'sinking-receipt','The Sinking Receipt','an invented Grassrivers roadside bait shack','Jason: navy work shirt, grey jeans; Lucia: cream shirt, teal waterproof jacket, black jeans','one red tackle box containing sealed medicine packets and a folded receipt',
"Jason paid for a box that was supposed to contain cash. Inside, Lucia found somebody's emergency instead.|Sealed medicine packets filled the tackle box. A folded receipt named a clinic beyond the flooded road.|Their seller was already gone. So was the money Jason had borrowed to make this deal happen.|Lucia turned the receipt over. The delivery fee was unpaid. The medicine had never belonged to him.|Jason stared toward the highway. He could still sell the box and recover something before morning came.|Then the shack radio mentioned stranded patients. Lucia closed the lid, gently, as though somebody could hear.|They left the box with a relief volunteer beside the public shelter, asking only for a receipt.|Jason folded that receipt into his empty wallet. For once, proof of losing money felt worth keeping.",
"Jason sets closed red tackle box on bait counter|Lucia opens lid: intact sealed packets, no loose pills|Jason sees seller's empty doorway; Lucia remains beside box|Macro receipt front, fingers on edges, back revealed in next angle|Jason at window looks toward highway; closed box between heroes|Lucia closes lid while small shack radio glows on shelf|Anonymous volunteer receives closed box at public shelter desk|Jason slips folded new receipt into empty wallet; Lucia nearby",
'misrepresented cargo creates a restitution choice')

add(3,'bell-in-the-rain','The Bell in the Rain','an invented Grassrivers abandoned visitor pier','Jason: tan canvas jacket, charcoal tee, jeans; Lucia: dark green shirt, black raincoat, jeans','one brass service bell bolted to a counter; one empty canvas satchel',
"The bell rang three times from the empty pier. Jason thought their contact had finally arrived tonight.|Lucia saw nobody behind the visitor counter, only an empty satchel somebody had carefully placed beneath it.|A handwritten sign promised payment for bringing that bag to a waiting truck beyond the mangrove bend.|Jason lifted the strap. The bell rang again, although both his hands were right there in sight.|Lucia followed the sound upward. Rainwater filled a broken gutter, tipping a loose board against the bell.|They had spent an hour answering weather. Their contact had never come. Neither had the promised money.|Jason put the satchel back. Lucia tore down the sign before another desperate stranger could believe it.|As they left, the bell rang once more. This time, neither of them turned around to answer.",
"Heroes approach empty pier counter as bolted brass bell vibrates|Lucia peers behind counter, empty canvas satchel on lower shelf|Handwritten payment sign beside trail to distant parked truck, no occupants visible|Jason lifts empty bag by strap; Lucia watches bell|Upward view of rain-filled gutter tipping loose board onto bell|Wide deserted pier; heroes alone with open empty satchel|Lucia removes sign, Jason returns bag to same lower shelf|Heroes exit away from pier, bell small in background",
'natural sound mistaken for an arranged signal')

add(3,'white-line','The White Line','an invented Grassrivers flooded causeway rest stop','Jason: faded denim jacket, black tee, dark trousers; Lucia: amber windbreaker, black jeans','one chalk stripe across road and one silver hard case with empty interior',
"The instructions said stop before the white line. Jason stopped with his front tire exactly on it.|Lucia stepped out and saw the line disappearing beneath brown water. It was chalk, already washing away.|Their buyer waited on the far side, holding up cash and waving them forward through the flood.|Jason raised the silver case. The buyer raised his money higher. Neither could hear the other's warning.|Then a fence post drifted sideways between them. Lucia saw the current before Jason saw the missing road.|She pulled him back from the edge. The empty case slipped away, turning slowly in the rain.|Across the gap, the buyer lowered his cash. He finally stepped backward too, both hands suddenly empty.|They lost the case and kept the car. The white line vanished. Lucia remembered exactly where it was.",
"Stationary charcoal sedan front left tire touches chalk stripe on dry pavement|Lucia exits passenger side, stripe ends at muddy water|Distant anonymous buyer on separate bank holds cash above waist|Jason stands safely at stripe holding closed silver case; sedan parked behind|Fence post drifts laterally over missing road gap, Lucia points|Lucia pulls Jason one step back; case floats away beyond reach|Buyer retreats on far bank, cash lowered; water separates all people|Wide car still parked on dry bank, heroes together, chalk dissolving",
'a boundary warning acquires its true meaning')

add(3,'two-dry-chairs','Two Dry Chairs','an invented Grassrivers storm shelter laundry','Jason: grey hoodie, dark trousers; Lucia: maroon shirt, navy rain shell, black trousers','one blue laundry sack holding wet cash; two plastic chairs',
"Two dry chairs stood in the shelter. Jason put their bag on one and guarded the other.|Lucia watched rain spill through the doorway around a woman carrying a child against her shoulder.|The woman asked for nothing. She stood beside a broken dryer, rocking slowly while the thunder moved closer.|Jason tightened his hand around the sack. Every wet bill inside had cost them something they couldn't explain.|Lucia lifted the bag off the chair. Jason looked ready to argue, then saw the child's bare feet.|They stood by the door while the woman sat. Water from their sleeves gathered beneath the chairs.|When daylight came, the woman thanked them for the seats. She never asked what was in the bag.|Jason carried their money outside. Lucia carried the empty chairs back from the leak. Both finally felt lighter.",
"Jason places blue tied sack on left plastic chair, right chair empty|Lucia looks toward anonymous woman with sleeping child in doorway|Woman waits beside broken dryer, heroes foreground|Jason's hand on tied wet sack; no visible cash outside sack|Lucia lifts sack, child feet in distant frame, no distress closeup|Woman and child use two chairs, heroes stand near doorway with sack|Dawn at shelter door, woman nods thanks; bag stays with Jason|Jason exits carrying sack; Lucia shifts chairs clear of ceiling leak",
'resource sharing instead of suspicion')

add(3,'borrowed-thunder','Borrowed Thunder','an invented Grassrivers kayak rental shed','Jason: black waterproof jacket, grey tee, blue jeans; Lucia: rust overshirt, navy trousers','one black duffel with bundled cash; one nailed shutter',
"Lucia heard gunfire outside the shed. Jason killed the lamp, and their stolen money disappeared into darkness.|They crouched beside rental kayaks while three sharp cracks rolled through the rain from the tree line.|Jason wanted the back door. Lucia held his sleeve. The same three cracks came again, exactly spaced.|She watched a loose shutter jump against its frame. A rotten branch was striking it with each gust.|Jason laughed once, too loudly. Beyond the shutter, a real flashlight swept across the flooded rental yard.|They stayed still. An anonymous caretaker secured the branch, checked the empty kayaks, and walked back home.|Only then did Lucia relight the lamp. The money was still there. Jason's confidence hadn't come back.|At dawn they left the duffel for its owner at the shed. Fear had finally named the price.",
"Lucia reacts, Jason switches off small battery lamp; black duffel at feet|Heroes crouch behind stacked empty kayaks, closed duffel between them|Lucia grips Jason sleeve, both face shutter at right wall|Close shutter and branch striking outside; no weapon or shooter|Flashlight beam crosses yard through shutter gap, heroes remain concealed|Anonymous caretaker secures branch outside while kayaks remain empty|Lucia switches lamp on; duffel unchanged, Jason subdued|Dawn empty shed, duffel on counter with return note; heroes departing",
'false alarm reveals emotional cost; restitution resolves guilt')

add(3,'watermark','The Watermark','an invented Grassrivers fishing lodge lobby','Jason: olive shirt, charcoal jeans; Lucia: blue denim shirt, black rain jacket, dark trousers','one water-damaged handwritten debt ledger and one desk lamp',
"Lucia was told the ledger could erase their debt. Then rain erased the number beside their names.|Jason spread the soaked pages beneath a desk lamp. Half the ink had bled into a blue cloud.|The anonymous collector tapped a blank space and demanded twice what they owed, as though weather created interest.|Lucia didn't argue over the number. She turned to an earlier page where another payment had dried completely.|Her signature sat beneath it, sharp. The collector had counted that same debt twice before.|Jason slid both pages into the light. For once, the man stopped explaining and started looking.|They left with the ledger's signed receipt, their balance crossed out. The collector kept his ruined book.|Outside, Jason asked whether she'd remembered that payment. Lucia smiled. She remembered every time somebody called her forgetful.",
"Lucia opens wet handwritten ledger on lodge desk|Jason fans pages beneath amber desk lamp without tearing binding|Anonymous collector stands opposite desk, points at washed-out amount|Lucia turns back to earlier dry page, Jason watches|Insert old signed payment entry, readable overlay added in edit|Jason angles open ledger toward lamp; collector goes quiet|Collector signs separate receipt, Lucia receives it; ledger stays on desk|Heroes under lodge awning, Lucia holds folded receipt, rain beyond",
'document inconsistency overturns a dishonest debt claim')

add(4,'room-for-one','Room for One More','an invented Port Gellhorn freight elevator lobby','Jason: navy bomber jacket, white tee, black jeans; Lucia: dark red shirt, charcoal coat, trousers','one dented handcart with a sealed grey cash case; elevator capacity plate',
"The elevator would carry the money or Jason. Lucia read the weight limit and made him step off.|They dragged the case through a deserted warehouse while the storm shut every street around them.|Jason thought she was choosing the score. He watched her wheel it inside and press the upper floor.|Lucia came straight back out. The doors closed on the case alone, rattling upward through the building.|Their buyer's voice echoed from above, demanding they join him. Jason finally noticed the damaged elevator cables overhead.|Lucia pointed toward the stairs. They climbed slowly, hearing metal complain behind the walls with every step.|The elevator stopped between floors. The buyer shouted at the locked doors while Jason and Lucia passed him.|Outside, Jason asked about their cut. Lucia counted them both with two fingers. The whole amount, she said.",
"Lucia reads capacity plate, Jason steps back from elevator, handcart inside doorway|Wide rain-dark warehouse lobby, grey sealed case on dented cart|Jason outside lift watches Lucia press upper button|Lucia exits to Jason; lift doors close on cart and case only|Upward inspection of frayed external cable housing, no operational detail|Heroes start public stairs at left, empty landing ahead|Upper landing, anonymous buyer at stalled lift doors, heroes pass on stairs|Heroes beneath warehouse awning, Lucia raises two fingers, rain beyond",
'apparent betrayal becomes a safety choice')

add(4,'last-bid','The Last Bid','an invented Port Gellhorn salvage auction canopy','Jason: brown leather jacket, dark tee, jeans; Lucia: teal overshirt, black trousers','one brass boat compass in glass display; numbered paper bid paddle',
"Jason bid their last thousand on a broken compass. Lucia waited until the auctioneer asked him why.|The compass had belonged to a boat they once abandoned. He recognized the crack across its brass rim.|Lucia remembered that night differently: empty fuel, cold rain, and Jason promising they would never gamble rent again.|An anonymous bidder raised a paddle. Jason raised his. The compass needle stayed stuck, pointing nowhere.|Lucia laid their motel key beside his hand. One rectangle weighed more than the whole glass cabinet.|Jason lowered his paddle. The other bidder won, looked delighted, and carried the broken compass into the rain.|Outside, Lucia expected an argument. Jason asked whether their room still had the heater that sounded like thunder.|It did. They bought groceries on the way back. For once, getting home was the direction he chose.",
"Jason raises numbered paper paddle beneath salvage auction canopy, Lucia beside him|Insert cracked brass compass under glass, needle fixed northeast|Lucia watches Jason, folded raincoat over chair back|Anonymous bidder raises paddle across aisle, Jason responds|Lucia lays plain motel key on table beside Jason hand|Jason lowers paddle; anonymous winner receives compass in padded box|Heroes exit canopy side by side into light rain|Heroes carry plain grocery bag toward warm motel corridor, no car",
'sentimental bidding versus keeping a shared promise')

add(4,'wet-paint','Wet Paint','an invented Port Gellhorn shuttered body-shop courtyard','Jason: grey denim shirt, dark trousers; Lucia: olive jacket, white tee, black jeans','one stationary cream coupe with fresh blue door; one orange pawn envelope',
"Their getaway car came back with a blue door. Jason had paid for cream, and Lucia wanted answers.|The painter shrugged and pointed at the invoice. One door replaced. Color matching had never been included.|Jason called it a mistake. Lucia called it a landmark. Everyone they'd passed would remember this car.|Then the painter mentioned the owner who'd collected his cream door yesterday, smiling through a mouthful of excuses.|Lucia opened their envelope. The ownership papers were photocopies. Their car had been sold twice.|Jason looked at the keys. The engine still worked. That made walking away feel harder than it should.|They left the coupe with the shop and filed their claim from the bus stop across the street.|The bus arrived blue and cream. Lucia laughed first. This time, Jason knew exactly what their fare bought.",
"Stationary cream coupe with single blue driver door, both heroes outside|Anonymous painter points to invoice on clipboard, coupe behind|Lucia examines mismatched panel, Jason holds keys|Painter gestures toward empty wall door rack, no second car shown|Lucia opens orange envelope revealing photocopied ownership sheets|Jason holds key above open palm, engine off, driver seat visibly empty|Coupe stays shop with keys on desk; heroes at bus stop using phone|Blue and cream public bus stops; heroes board with no luggage",
'visible repair exposes a fraudulent sale; legal exit')

add(4,'unclaimed','Unclaimed','an invented Port Gellhorn bus depot lost-property office','Jason: dark green jacket, grey shirt, jeans; Lucia: tan raincoat, black trousers','one purple suitcase holding theatre costumes and an embroidered child drawing',
"The suitcase was purple, just like the message said. The clown inside wasn't part of their deal.|Lucia lifted a sequined jacket. Jason found giant shoes, stage makeup, and a drawing embroidered into the lining.|They had taken the wrong case from a crowded depot, certain every purple suitcase belonged to their contact.|A public announcement asked for missing theatre costumes. Tomorrow's children's performance had already sold every seat available.|Jason checked the clock. Their real pickup window was closing. Lucia zipped the costume case shut anyway.|They carried it to lost property, where a performer pressed both hands against the purple lid in relief.|Their contact stopped answering. No case, no payment. Jason sat beside Lucia beneath the departures board, silent.|Then he found one silver sequin on his sleeve. She grinned. First honest tip we'd earned all week.",
"Lucia opens purple suitcase on bench, clown costume visible, no person inside|Jason lifts giant shoe, Lucia holds sequined jacket, embroidered drawing on lining|Wide busy depot with anonymous extras, purple case at heroes' feet|Depot announcement speaker above poster for fictional children's theatre|Jason checks clock, Lucia closes zipper with all costume pieces inside|Heroes hand case to anonymous relieved performer at lost property desk|Heroes empty handed seated below departures board, Jason phone screen dark|Close silver sequin on Jason sleeve; Lucia smiles beside him",
'mistaken luggage and opportunity cost of returning it')

add(4,'window-seat','The Window Seat','an invented Port Gellhorn all-night diner','Jason: black shirt, grey canvas jacket, jeans; Lucia: burgundy blouse, dark raincoat, trousers','one red booth, one untouched coffee, one folded anonymous threat note',
"Lucia picked the window seat so nobody could surprise her. Jason watched her reflection.|The note on their table said someone inside knew what they'd done. Neither recognized a face there.|A man beside the counter stood up. Jason tensed. The man only asked the waitress for extra napkins.|Lucia saw headlights stop outside. Her coffee trembled. A delivery driver came in carrying a stack of bread.|Finally Jason unfolded the note again. Beneath the threat, tiny print advertised the diner's murder mystery night tomorrow.|Lucia read it twice. The waitress had placed one on every table, between the sugar and the ketchup.|Jason started laughing, then stopped when Lucia didn't. She was looking at her hands around the untouched coffee.|They paid and walked home together. Neither needed to explain why an ordinary invitation had sounded so believable.",
"Lucia sits at window side of red booth, Jason opposite watching reflection|Folded note between coffee and ketchup; anonymous diners reflected behind|Anonymous man at counter asks waitress, heroes remain in booth|Bread delivery driver enters, headlights of parked delivery van outside|Jason unfolds complete printed promotion; add legible text in edit|Lucia reads, identical promotions visible on neighboring empty tables|Lucia hands cradle untouched white coffee cup, Jason expression softens|Heroes walk wet sidewalk away from diner, booth now empty behind glass",
'guilty interpretation of harmless evidence')

add(4,'low-tide-money','Low Tide Money','an invented Port Gellhorn derelict marina payphone shelter','Jason: ochre work jacket, navy tee, jeans; Lucia: charcoal hoodie, olive rain shell, black trousers','one transparent waterproof pouch holding damp banknotes; bolted public donation box',
"Low tide returned the money Jason had thrown away. Lucia wished the harbor had kept its mouth shut.|The transparent pouch lay tangled beneath the public pier, where he'd dropped it after their argument.|They stood above it, waiting. Back then, keeping the money had meant breaking a promise.|Now their rent was late. Jason climbed down the public steps and lifted the pouch from the mud.|Lucia said nothing. He counted through the plastic, lips moving, already rebuilding the life that money could buy.|Then a marina worker pointed to the donation box for storm repairs. Its glass front held three coins.|Jason looked from the pouch to Lucia. He pushed it through the collection slot before reconsidering.|They walked home with wet shoes and the same overdue rent. This time, the money stayed thrown away.",
"Heroes look down from public pier at transparent pouch caught on exposed rung|Insert same pouch in mud, banknotes visible through plastic|Heroes stand shoulder to shoulder above low water|Jason uses public steps, safely lifts pouch, Lucia stays at top|Jason holds sealed pouch toward light, counts visible note edges|Anonymous worker gestures to bolted donation box with three coins behind glass|Jason slides entire flexible pouch into wide donation slot, Lucia witnesses|Heroes leave along waterfront on foot, donation pouch visible in box behind",
'temptation returns; voluntary public restitution closes the conflict')

add(4,'the-empty-stage','The Empty Stage','an invented Port Gellhorn closed waterfront cinema','Jason: slate shirt, brown rain jacket, dark jeans; Lucia: navy jacket, cream tee, black trousers','one small silver key on red ribbon; one red velvet stage curtain',
"The key opened nothing in the cinema. Jason tried twice before Lucia read the ribbon.|It carried a date, not a room number. Tonight. Their buyer promised a fortune for this key.|They entered through the public side lobby, where a caretaker packed posters into a box.|She recognized the ribbon immediately. The key belonged to a time capsule beneath the cinema's old stage.|Jason asked about the buyer. The caretaker showed them a demolition notice. Someone wanted the capsule before witnesses arrived.|Lucia set the key on the ticket counter. She asked when the neighborhood could come and watch it opened.|At noon, the caretaker answered. Jason left their number for an invitation and tore up the private offer.|The capsule held letters, not cash. They heard them read aloud the next day. Nobody had to steal those voices.",
"Jason holds silver key at closed cinema front, Lucia studies red ribbon|Macro ribbon printed with date, silver key hangs consistently teeth down|Heroes enter open public side lobby, anonymous caretaker with poster box|Caretaker gestures toward stage floor, red curtain drawn to left|Demolition notice on ticket counter, heroes read, key in Lucia hand|Lucia lays key and attached ribbon on counter, talks to caretaker|Jason tears private offer paper above waste bin; key stays counter|Next day public gathering before open capsule letters on stage, heroes seated",
'private extraction replaced by community witnessing',timeline='linear with next-day epilogue')

