REVISIONS = {
'white-line': "He said stop before the white line. We're on it. Tell me that counts as before.|Jason, look where it ends. That's water over there. The line is chalk, and it's washing away.|He's waving the cash again. Maybe the road just dips. We could leave the car and walk.|That fence post isn't floating toward us. It's moving sideways. There's a current where the road should be.|I can't see the edge anymore. Take my sleeve. Forget the case, just tell me where to step.|Back toward my voice. One step. Good. The case is gone. Leave it. Keep your eyes on me.|He's backing away too. He didn't know. All that money, and neither side could see the missing road.|Then we both got a warning in time. Keep the car here. We'll wait for somebody who knows.",
'last-bid': "A thousand for a broken compass. That's our boat's compass. I know that cracked rim.|I know it too. I remember empty fuel, cold rain, and you promising we wouldn't gamble rent again.|We left so much behind that night. Getting one piece back might make it feel different.|Then hold this key. Our room is real. Its heater works. What direction does that compass even point?|Nowhere. The needle's stuck. That man's bidding again. If I lower my paddle, he gets to keep it.|He gets a broken compass. We get tonight, groceries, and a door we can close. Let him have it.|All right. He's won. Keep the key, though. I'd rather carry the groceries. Does that heater sound terrible?|Like thunder in a tin bucket. We'll complain about it all night. That's how we'll know we're home.",
'borrowed-thunder': "I returned the money at dawn because a tree branch frightened me. Lucia won't let me forget.|Hours earlier, three cracks hit the rental shed. I killed the lamp and crouched beside our stolen duffel.|I wanted the back door. Lucia held my sleeve. Three cracks repeated, in identical rhythm.|She pointed up. Wind bent a rotten branch against a loose shutter. I laughed before I could stop myself.|Then a flashlight crossed the yard. An anonymous caretaker walked past us, close enough to hear us.|He secured the branch and left. We stayed frozen beside the kayaks until his light vanished.|Lucia switched the lamp on. The cash was still there. I couldn't remember what it was supposed to fix.|At dawn, we left it for its owner. The branch hadn't threatened my life. It had shown me mine.",
'watermark': "I remembered every payment. The collector remembered the ones that let him charge us again.|Rain had soaked his ledger before we reached the lodge. Our balance looked like a blue cloud.|He tapped the blank space and demanded double. Jason spread the pages under the lamp, seeking our number.|I let the man talk. Each explanation gave me time to find the entry he hoped I'd forgotten.|Three pages earlier, my signature sat beneath a payment, dry. He had counted the same debt twice.|Jason moved both entries into the light. The collector stopped explaining. I waited until he offered a receipt.|He crossed out our balance and signed. We left his ruined ledger behind. I kept the receipt flat.|Outside, Jason asked whether I'd remembered that payment. Of course. People always get generous when they call me forgetful.",
'room-for-one': "The buyer wanted midnight delivery. One minute past, he tried to keep Jason and Lucia's fee too.|Earlier, Lucia had read the freight elevator's weight limit. The case could ride safely. Both couriers couldn't.|Jason stepped out beside her. They sent the sealed case upstairs alone, then climbed the warehouse's stairs.|Above them, the delivery bell rang. Through a window, Lucia saw the clock: one minute before midnight.|They reached their buyer. His smile was enormous. Late arrivals didn't get paid, he told them.|Lucia pointed at his delivery receipt. The building's caretaker had signed for the case when the elevator bell rang.|He'd bought delivery, not their company. Jason held out his hand. The buyer counted their full fee into it.|They took the stairs down together. Lucia kept the receipt. Jason complained that work had too many steps."
}
